     1	---
     2	name: dxb-team2
     3	description: Use when the CEO hands the construction a job — the default construction door. One lead (the session) scores the job, writes the done-list, chooses who writes (itself, a fork or medium helpers) and verifies every piece; a fresh, blind, read-only GPT-6.1 Sol auditor checks every job once, at a depth set by its score; Fable 5.1 is consulted once, at the end, on a critical job only. Trigger `/dxb-team2 <the job in one sentence>`.
     4	---
     5	
     6	# The team — one lead, the hands it chooses, one blind auditor, Fable at the end
     7	
     8	The construction's default door since 2026-09-28 <!-- CEO-OK: dxb-team2-default-door-2026-09-28 -->, built against waste, not against checking. The laws it obeys are in `.claude/CLAUDE.md`;
     9	it opens `dxb-verify` and `dxb-close-row`.
    10	
    11	## 1. The shape
    12	
    13	```
    14	            THE CEO'S SENTENCE — the approach talked through with him
    15	                    │
    16	            SCORE CARD (4 axes, 0-8) + DONE-LIST + budget
    17	                    │
    18	     ┌──────────────┼──────────────────────────┐
    19	  LIGHT (0-2)   NORMAL (3-5)             CRITICAL (6-8)
    20	     │              │              the lead plans → Sol reads the plan
    21	     │              │              → the lead closes its gaps
    22	     └──────────────┼──────────────────────────┘
    23	                    ▼
    24	     THE PLAN, in his language → the CEO looks at it once → his yes
    25	                    │
    26	     ARRANGEMENT (§3) — the lead, a fork, or medium helpers write; the lead verifies
    27	                    │
    28	     SOL AUDITOR — blind, read-only, ONE pass:  medium │ high │ xhigh
    29	                    │
    30	     finding → a fork or a helper fixes → the lead verifies; no second Sol round
    31	     disagreement → a test decides → else the lead (critical: Fable, in its one call)
    32	                    │
    33	     BATTERY once · dependants re-measured · FABLE once, critical only · commit · report
    34	```
    35	
    36	## 2. Seats
    37	
    38	| Seat | Who | Does | Never |
    39	|---|---|---|---|
    40	| **Lead** | the session — model and effort are the CEO's choice (`high` is the measured sweet spot); design, plan and architecture turns at `max` through `dxb-design-max` (§4 PLAN) | scores the job, writes the done-list, chooses the arrangement (§3), writes code itself or through the hands it chose, verifies every piece and every fix, runs the battery, commits, reports | approves its own work; commits a piece it has not verified; writes code at Opus `xhigh` (FrontierCode: 51.4 %, the bottom of the curve); asks the CEO a technical question |
    41	| **Auditor** | GPT-6.1 Sol <!-- CEO-OK: auditor-sol-6-1-2026-10-01 --> through `scripts/governance/refuter.sh --card <file>` — read-only by tool | one blind pass per job (§5) — there is no second round <!-- CEO-OK: sol-single-pass-fixes-by-helper-2026-10-03 --> | writes; audits the same job twice; sees a verdict or the lead's reasoning; is called as bare `codex` (the base config writes everywhere) |
    42	| **Advisor** | Fable 5.1 | once per job, at the end — before "done" — and only on a critical job (card 6-8): reads the finished work with Sol's findings and, in the same call, rules on a disagreement no test settled <!-- CEO-OK: fable-at-the-end-on-important-jobs-2026-10-04 --> | writes code; is consulted before or during the work, on a repeated error, or on a light or normal job — the advisor tool's own advice to call it before substantive work does not apply here |
    43	| **Fork** | the lead's own copy (`subagent_type: "fork"`), on the lead's model | writes the job, or a part of it, with the lead's whole context (§3) | commits |
    44	| **Helper** | `helper` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`, read-only) | reads code, documents or the outside world for the lead, beside the others | writes |
    45	| **Writing helper** | `helper-writer` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`) | edits and tests a piece the lead specified; fixes Sol's findings | commits; widens the piece — the lead commits only what it verified <!-- CEO-OK: helpers-write-code-under-lead-verification-2026-10-03 --> |
    46	| **Reader** | a one-shot subagent (`scout`, or `Explore`) | a wide search or read whose text would swell the lead's context | writes; is resumed (its cache dies at 5 min) |
    47	| **Escalation writer** | `builder` (Opus 5.5 · `max`), one-shot, description `guarded:` when the path is guarded | only after the same piece failed twice at the lead's level | is opened for a routine fix |
    48	| **Design eye** | `design-eye` (Fable 5.1) | reads a surface the CEO will see | writes |
    49	
    50	**Fallback auditor.** Sol unavailable (quota, error, timeout): on a light or normal job a fresh
    51	one-shot Opus 5.5 `high` subagent audits and the record says *"audited by Opus instead of Sol, because
    52	…"*; on a critical job the lead waits for Sol's 5-hour window and works on something else. No job
    53	closes unaudited; the auditor is never swapped silently.
    54	
    55	## 3. The score card — written before a line of code
    56	
    57	Four axes, 0-2 each (the depth rule from the outside review of 2026-09-28, weighed and kept):
    58	
    59	| Axis | 0 | 1 | 2 |
    60	|---|---|---|---|
    61	| Blast radius | one function | one subsystem | several subsystems |
    62	| Risk | copy, layout | business logic | money · database · security · approval · governance · agents |
    63	| Reasoning | deterministic | a few edge cases | stateful, concurrent, agentic, emergent |
    64	| Ambiguity | exact | some reading | unclear or conflicting |
    65	
    66	**0-2 light · 3-5 normal · 6-8 critical.** The card (axes, total, class, auditor effort and why) goes
    67	into the commit body.
    68	
    69	**A machine holds the card** (*"tmm makineyi de kur"* <!-- CEO-OK: score-card-gate-2026-10-01 -->).
    70	The card is a file (`job:` · `range:` · `blast:` · `risk:` · `reasoning:` · `ambiguity:`) handed to
    71	`refuter.sh --card <file>`; `scripts/governance/audit-card.mjs` refuses an audit without one, checks
    72	that the card's range is real and changes something, refuses any forwarded option that could
    73	override the effort, model, sandbox or servers,
    74	sets the auditor's effort, refuses an `--effort` beneath it, shows Sol the card for information (§6), and logs
    75	every launch to `~/.local/state/dxb/audit-cards.log` — the class budgets of §7 are summed from it.
    76	
    77	**The arrangement — who writes** (*"bu yazdığın plan oklenebilir. ve 1-2 işten sonra ölçümde
    78	yapılabilir."* <!-- CEO-OK: orchestration-three-arrangements-lead-chooses-2026-10-03 -->). Before BUILD
    79	the lead chooses one per job and writes it on the card as `arrangement:`, with its why:
    80	
    81	| Arrangement | Who writes | Beside it |
    82	|---|---|---|
    83	| **Fork** | the fork — the lead's own copy, with its whole context | — |
    84	| **Team** | `helper-writer` edits and runs the tests | a `helper` reads the code, a `helper` reads documents or researches; the lead gathers their results and decides |
    85	| **Hybrid** | the fork | `helper`s read and research |
    86	
    87	The core's *"the session writes it"* stands beside them: a piece the lead writes itself is written
    88	`arrangement: lead`, with its why. At the job's end the card carries, under `## Measured`, the minutes of
    89	each phase, the new tokens and their list-price cost — `node .planning/quick/20261003-runtime-isolation/usage.mjs <transcript> --from <ISO>`
    90	sums them for the lead's transcript and for each subagent's (`<session>/subagents/`), priced at the rates
    91	`cost.py` beside it uses — and Sol's findings. After one or two real jobs these records show which
    92	arrangement suits which job; there is no separate A/B test. The 50 % handover gate (§8) holds in every
    93	arrangement.
    94	
    95	## 4. The loop
    96	
    97	```
    98	INTAKE    → the job in ONE sentence, provable by measurement; the CEO's words verbatim; what is his;
    99	            what it excludes, what it touches, what contradicts what across documents and code,
   100	            the real unknowns that could change the plan — reported to him in his language
   101	            before any proposal; no generic software advice, no documents restated.
   102	SCORE     → the card (§3); `dxb-quota` read once — the week's headroom and pace.
   103	PLAN      → the approach is talked through with the CEO first; the lead then writes the plan.
   104	            A critical one: Sol reads the PLAN (marked draft) → the lead closes its gaps. The
   105	            plan goes to the CEO once, in his language, and
   106	            no file changes before his yes. It does not come back to him after Sol's read. The
   107	            plan names the scope, the surfaces and files it touches, the ordered steps, the data
   108	            and interface effects, migration and rollback where relevant, how it is verified, and
   109	            the risks.
   110	            Every turn of this talk and of the plan — design, plan, architecture — runs at `max`:
   111	            the lead invokes `dxb-design-max` as its first step; a skill holds max for one turn,
   112	            so its project hook `dxb-design-max.py` reminds it on each of his messages and the
   113	            lead calls it on every one, whatever it asks; only his yes closes the mode
   114	            <!-- CEO-OK: design-max-only-his-yes-closes-2026-10-04 --> and the job goes on at the session's level
   115	            <!-- CEO-OK: design-plan-architecture-at-max-2026-10-03 --> <!-- CEO-OK: design-max-skill-every-turn-2026-10-04 -->.
   116	DONE-LIST → numbered, each item a command and its expected output, written BEFORE the code.
   117	BUILD     → root cause before fix; new code is proven by a test that failed before it existed. The
   118	            writers write and run the job's own tests — the approved scope, completely: no stub, no
   119	            placeholder, no silent narrowing or widening; a discovery that would change the scope,
   120	            architecture, security, data integrity or the outcome stops the work and is reported.
   121	            The change matches the surrounding code — naming, structure, comment density, error
   122	            handling, idiom. The hands are the arrangement's (§3); whoever writes, the lead verifies
   123	            each piece — its failing-first test, a reading against the registered sources — before
   124	            it is committed.
   125	AUDIT     → Sol, blind (§5), one pass at the class's effort (§6).
   126	            A — blocks · B — repaired in this same pass · C — older than this work → stays in the
   127	            job's own folder (the audit report); never the board — a row opens only on his word.
   128	FIX       → a fork or a `helper-writer` fixes each finding; the lead verifies it — the finding's own
   129	            failing case now green, what the fix touched re-measured. There is no second Sol round
   130	            <!-- CEO-OK: sol-single-pass-fixes-by-helper-2026-10-03 -->.
   131	DISPUTE   → the lead says a finding is wrong: the lead runs the evidence Sol gave with it —
   132	            it fails → real, fixed; it holds → the finding drops, the reason is recorded.
   133	            No test can decide it (a design question): the lead decides and records why; on a
   134	            critical job Fable rules on it in its one call at the end (§2).
   135	            The lead has the last word. The CEO reads the line in the report; he is not asked.
   136	BRAKE     → the job passes its class budget (§7): stop at a clean break, find why it grew,
   137	            and ask the CEO before going on.
   138	JUDGE     → battery ONCE, the dependants of every changed thing re-measured and printed; on a
   139	            critical job Fable's one call, before "done" (§2); one commit per phase.
   140	TELL      → the CEO, in his language, under three headings: Sizi bekleyen · Değişen · Bulunan.
   141	            An empty heading is not written; the evidence stays in the work until he asks; never
   142	            a technical question. A defect fixed inside the job is not listed (the commit carries
   143	            it); what is found outside it goes under Bulunan with a recommendation, he decides.
   144	RECORD    → STATE (the contradicted sentence goes, LAW A), the board row, rulers green.
   145	```
   146	
   147	**What reaches the CEO as a question:** starting any job, its plan (once), money out, a contract, an
   148	identity step, the Islamic boundaries, a job outgrowing its budget, and an order of his
   149	that truly reads two ways — each with a recommendation, answerable in one word. Technical choices
   150	inside a job he already said yes to are the lead's. The plan's single trip and the three-heading
   151	report are his words of 2026-10-03 <!-- CEO-OK: plan-comes-to-him-once-2026-10-03 --> <!-- CEO-OK: job-report-three-headings-2026-10-03 -->.
   152	
   153	## 5. The auditor's brief — blind, not in the dark
   154	
   155	| Sol sees | Sol does not see |
   156	|---|---|
   157	| the CEO's sentence, verbatim | "Opus / Fable found it correct" |
   158	| the plan and its design decisions **as facts** ("this door is closed on purpose") | the lead's reasoning or summary |
   159	| the done-list | "tests are green" or any other summary |
   160	| the diff | a previous auditor's verdict |
   161	| test output, **raw and whole** | |
   162	
   163	Its first question on every job: *does this done-list prove what the CEO's sentence asked for?* — the
   164	lead wrote the exam; the auditor checks the exam too. **Sol queries the construction engine itself**
   165	through its one tool `sql_read` (`scripts/governance/sol-db-mcp.mjs`, role `sol_reader`, job 1
   166	2026-09-28) — the brief tells it so, and tells it to read that file before trusting it; the lead never
   167	hands over a count the auditor can take. Sol may ask for any other read-only command; the lead runs it
   168	and returns the raw output. Sol's sandbox has no network, so the database-writing test suites are run
   169	by the lead and handed over whole. Sol audits once; there is no reply round. Output per claim:
   170	
   171	```text
   172	Claim / done-list item:
   173	Verdict: REFUTED | STANDS AFTER ATTEMPTED REFUTATION | UNVERIFIED
   174	Finding: file:line, concrete scenario, impact
   175	Evidence: the command or artifact and its decisive result
   176	Correction required:
   177	```
   178	
   179	## 6. Effort routing — the charts, read from the CEO's own screenshots 2026-09-27
   180	
   181	| Opus 5.5 | medium | high | xhigh | max |
   182	|---|---|---|---|---|
   183	| FrontierCode (mergeable code) | **54.6 % · USD 0.80** | 54.0 % · USD 1.09 | 51.4 % · USD 2.25 | 54.4 % · USD 6.19 |
   184	| Terminal-Bench 4.0 | ≈ 58 % | **64.2 % · USD 3.88** | 66.4 % · USD 7.35 | 64.8 % · USD 11.24 |
   185	| CursorBench 4.0 | 52.5 % | ≈ 56 % | ≈ 56 % | 57.8 % |
   186	
   187	- **Writing code:** the lead's level (`high`). Opus `xhigh` is never used for code — the bottom of the
   188	  FrontierCode curve (the CEO's word, 2026-09-28). `max` only after the same piece failed twice at
   189	  `high`: a medium try plus a high retry (USD 1.89) is under a third of one `max` run (USD 6.19) and
   190	  passes the same auditor. Prices here are written USD, never with a dollar sign: opened with a job
   191	  sentence, this door has a dollar sign and a digit replaced by the sentence's words (measured
   192	  2026-10-04).
   193	- **Auditor (Sol):** light `medium` · normal `high` · critical `xhigh`. The first critical job runs
   194	  `high` and `xhigh` on the same diff side by side; that number keeps or moves the critical level.
   195	- `audit-card.mjs` sets the auditor's level from the card; the lead may raise it, never lower it. The card is the lead's judgment — no machine can tell a dangerous file (the CEO,
   196	  2026-10-01: *"sistem tahmin edemez onu sadece sen bilirsin"*). **Sol audits at the level the card
   197	  gives and never re-grades it** <!-- CEO-OK: auditor-never-regrades-2026-10-03 --> — checking the
   198	  done-list (§5) is auditing the work; grading the job is the lead's.
   199	
   200	## 7. The brake and the bill
   201	
   202	- Class budgets are set from the first three jobs (light · normal · critical) — measured, never
   203	  guessed. Until then the lead reads `dxb-quota` at the start and at each phase end.
   204	- Nothing is logged by hand. Every token is already in the transcripts
   205	  (`~/.claude/projects/-home-dxb-DxB-Global-OS/**/*.jsonl`, `message.usage`) and the Codex rollouts
   206	  (`~/.codex/sessions/**`, `rate_limits`); when the CEO asks, one command sums them.
   207	
   208	## 8. Findings, long commands, context and handover
   209	
   210	- **A / B / C** and his law of 2026-09-22: a minor finding is repaired in the same pass.
   211	- **The 4-minute rules** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-cost-gate.py`): a command over 4 minutes is the
   212	  lead's; a subagent idle over 4 minutes is never resumed — its cache is the 5-minute one.
   213	- **The context gate** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-context-gate.py`, the CEO's numbers of
   214	  2026-09-26): hand over at ≥ 50 % used, or when used + the next phase's honest estimate > 55 %;
   215	  only at a clean break — no battery running, tree committed, report sent.
   216	- **The handover** is done by the engineer, never the CEO
   217	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-50-without-asking-2026-10-01 -->. A note in the scratchpad, in the author's
   218	  own voice (the job's place
   219	  in the whole first, then his words of this job verbatim, every phase with status and commit, the
   220	  traps met, the first message the successor sends; session-only orders marked as such; last, the
   221	  path of this session's own conversation — `~/.claude/projects/-home-dxb-DxB-Global-OS/$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.jsonl`
   222	  — with the line *if anything here is unclear, read the part you need there*), `wl-copy`'d
   223	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-carries-transcript-2026-10-03 -->. He is quoted verbatim — from the ledger, or
   224	  from this conversation marked so <!-- CEO-OK: handover-quotes-conversation-verbatim-2026-10-03 --> —
   225	  never composed in his first person.
   226	  The successor opens with the same model and effort as this session, in a VS Code editor-area
   227	  terminal, through `operator` (look first; if the CEO is typing, wait): `operator key ctrl+shift+p`
   228	  → `operator type "Terminal: Create New Terminal in Editor Area"` → `operator key Return` →
   229	  `operator shot` → paste with `operator key ctrl+shift+v` (`ctrl+v` does not reach the terminal;
   230	  `operator type` inverts case here, so a command line is pasted, never typed) the line
   231	  `systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --collect -p MemoryMax=16G -p MemorySwapMax=4G -- /home/dxb/.local/bin/claude --model <same> --effort <same> "$(cat <note>)"`
   232	  (`claude` is not on that terminal's PATH) → `operator shot`, read it → `operator key Return`.
   233	  Proof it is alive: `ListAgents` shows it and it answers.
   234	
   235	## 9. Owed measurements — the first jobs pay them
   236	
   237	- **The fork — its start measured 2026-10-03.** A forked subagent's first call read 203,676 tokens from
   238	  the lead's cache and wrote 1,200 (session 5ed74ad7): a fork does not re-read the lead's context. What
   239	  it reads after that is new, and it runs on the lead's model. Whether a fork, a team or a hybrid
   240	  pays on a real job is paid by the cards' measured fields (§3).
   241	- **Sol `high` vs `xhigh`** on the same critical diff: findings, false alarms, time, Plus usage.
   242	- **Plus share per audit:** `rate_limits` before and after each audit in the Codex rollout.
   243	- **The first critical job** was Sol's own database reach (built 2026-09-28, §5); its audit pays the
   244	  `high` vs `xhigh` measurement above.

